=E5=8F=91=E4=BB=B6=E4=BA=BA=EF=BC=9A=E6=9D=8E=E5=85=8B=E9=B9=8F(=E6=98=93=E6=B7=B1) <kepeng.lkp@antfin.com>

=E5=8F=91=E9=80=81=E6=97=B6=E9=97=B4=EF=BC=9A2019=E5=B9=B48=E6=9C=8819=E6=97=A5(=E6=98=9F=E6=9C=9F=E4=B8=80) 17:38

=E6=94=B6=E4=BB=B6=E4=BA=BA=EF=BC=9Asean+ietf <sean+ietf@sn3rd.com>; joe <joe@salowey.net>; caw <ca=
w@heapingbits.net>

=E6=8A=84=E3=80=80=E9=80=81=EF=BC=9Atls@ietf.org <tls@ietf.org>; "Blumenthal, Uri - 0553 - MITLL" <=
uri@ll.mit.edu>; Paul Yang <kaishen.yy=3D40alipay.com@dmarc.ietf.org>

=E4=B8=BB=E3=80=80=E9=A2=98=EF=BC=9ARe: [TLS] (offline) Re: Draft for SM cipher suites used in TLS1=
.3

=20

Hi WG chairs,

=20

Can we place the referenced documents in the TLS WG GitHub?

https://github.com/tlswg

=20

According to the discussion below, this can help people to read and underst=
and the referenced specifications.

=20

Thanks,

=20

Kind Regards

Kepeng=20

=20

=E5=8F=91=E4=BB=B6=E4=BA=BA: TLS <tls-bounces@ietf.org> =E4=BB=A3=E8=A1=A8 "Blumenthal, Uri - 0553 - MITL=
L" <uri@ll.mit.edu>
=E6=97=A5=E6=9C=9F: 2019=E5=B9=B48=E6=9C=8818=E6=97=A5 =E6=98=9F=E6=9C=9F=E6=97=A5 22:08
=E6=94=B6=E4=BB=B6=E4=BA=BA: Paul Yang <kaishen.yy=3D40alipay.com@dmarc.ietf.org>
=E6=8A=84=E9=80=81: "tls@ietf.org" <tls@ietf.org>
=E4=B8=BB=E9=A2=98: Re: [TLS] (offline) Re: Draft for SM cipher suites used in TLS1.3

=20

IMHO, placing the documents on GitHub would be perfect, and quite sufficien=
t.=20

=20

Please make sure to post the name of the repo here. ;/)

=20

I leave it to others to decide whether they'd want copies of today PDF file=
s sent to the mailing list directly.

Regards,

Uri

=20

Sent from my iPhone


On Aug 17, 2019, at 01:03, Paul Yang <kaishen.yy=3D40alipay.com@dmarc.ietf.or=
g> wrote:

Good points.

=20

The good news is that we have found some English PDFs of SM2, including the=
 missing part 1 and part 3. Will continue to find English translations of ot=
her SM standards mentioned in the draft.

=20

So, if we host a free website, say on Github or so, to provide those docs, =
is it convenient for you guys? Or should we just drop the   PDF files to thi=
s mailing list as attachments?

=20

On Aug 16, 2019, at 10:58 PM, Rene Struik <rstruik.ext@gmail.com> wrote:

=20

Arguably, "national" crypto specifications garnish more stature if these ar=
e made available to the pubic by that standard-setting body itself (who, the=
reby, acts as its authoritative source), without deference to a third party =
(that may, independently from the originator, enforce document control [e.g.=
, by effectuating technical changes or enforcing controlled dissemination]).

=20

Since your draft introducing SM cipher suites with TLS1.3 appeals to the au=
thority of a standard-setting authority, easy availability of the full and a=
ccredited technical documentation to the IETF community helps in scrutiny an=
d, e.g., evaluating claims in the security considerations section.

=20

On 8/16/2019 3:06 AM, Kepeng Li wrote:

Hi Rene and all,

=20

> Since the ISO documents are not available to the general=20
> public without payment, it would be helpful to have a freely available=20
> document (in English) from an authoritative source. Having such a=20
> reference available would be helpful to the IETF community (and=20
> researchers).
About the references to ISO documens, I think it is a general issue for IET=
F drafts.

=20

How does the other IETF drafts make the references to ISO documents? ISO do=
cuments are often referenced by IETF drafts.

=20

Thanks,

=20

Kind Regards

Kepeng

=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=
=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=
=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94=E2=80=94

Re: [TLS] Draft for SM cipher suites used in TLS1.3
Rene Struik <rstruik.ext@gmail.com> Thu, 15 August 2019 15:34 UTCShow heade=
r
Hi Paul:
=20
I tried and look up the documents GMT.0009-2012 and GBT.32918.5-2016 on=20
the (non-secured) websites you referenced, but only found Chinese=20
versions (and Chinese website navigation panels [pardon my poor language=20
skills here]). Since the ISO documents are not available to the general=20
public without payment, it would be helpful to have a freely available=20
document (in English) from an authoritative source. Having such a=20
reference available would be helpful to the IETF community (and=20
researchers). Please note that BSI provides its specifications in German=20
and English, so as to foster use/study by the community. If the Chinese=20
national algorithms would be available in similar form, this would serve=20
a similar purpose.
=20
FYI - I am interested in full details and some time last year I tried to=20
download specs, but only Parts 2, 4, and 5 were available [1], [2], [3],=20
not Parts 1 and 3.
=20
Best regards, Rene
=20
[1] China ECC - Public Key Cryptographic Algorithm SM2 Based on ECC -=20
Part 5 - Parameter Definition (SEMB, July 24, 2018)
[2] China ECC - Public Key Cryptographic Algorithm SM2 Based on ECC -=20
Part 2 - Digital Signature Algorithm (SEMB, July 24, 2018)
[3] China ECC - Public Key Cryptographic Algorithm SM2 Based on ECC -=20
Part 4 - Public Key Encryption Algorithm (SEMB, July 24, 2018)
=20
On 8/15/2019 10:16 AM, Paul Yang wrote:
> Hi all,
>=20
> I have submitted a new internet draft to introduce the SM cipher=20
> suites into TLS 1.3 protocol.
>=20
> https://tools.ietf.org/html/draft-yang-tls-tls13-sm-suites-00
>=20
> SM cryptographic algorithms are originally a set of Chinese national=20
> algorithms and now have been (or being) accepted by ISO as=20
> international standards, including SM2 signature algorithm, SM3 hash=20
> function and SM4 block cipher. These algorithms have already been=20
> supported some time ago by several widely used open source=20
> cryptographic libraries including OpenSSL, BouncyCastle, Botan, etc.
>=20
> Considering TLS1.3 is being gradually adopted in China's internet=20
> industry, it's important to have a normative definition on how to use=20
> the SM algorithms with TLS1.3, especially for the mobile internet=20
> scenario. Ant Financial is the company who develops the market leading=20
> mobile app 'Alipay' and supports payment services for Alibaba=20
> e-commerce business. We highly are depending on the new TLS1.3=20
> protocol for both performance and security purposes. We expect to have=20
> more deployment of TLS1.3 capable applications in China's internet=20
> industry by this standardization attempts.
>=20
> It's very appreciated to have comments from the IETF TLS list :-)
>=20
> Many thanks!
>=20
> _______________________________________________
> TLS mailing list
> TLS@ietf.org
> https://www.ietf.org/mailman/listinfo/tls
=20

=20
--=20
email: rstruik.ext@gmail.com | Skype: rstruik
cell: +1 (647) 867-5658 | US: +1 (415) 690-7363
_______________________________________________
